WEBVTT

N01a
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.650
The buyer files a chargeback.

N01b
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.450
The seller has days to answer.

N02
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.040
Miss it, and PayPal rules for the buyer.

N03
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.640
CaseClerk answers them for small PayPal sellers.

N04
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.510
Here it is, live on PayPal's sandbox.

N05
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.200
One click opens a real sandbox chargeback on a test order.

N06
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.990
Three minutes later, PayPal's webhook arrives, and CaseClerk checks the signature.

N07
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.150
A Claude agent reads the order and the tracking, then stops to ask the seller.

N08
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.780
A harder one: a $412.00 camera the buyer says isn't as described.

N09a
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.750
Tracking proves delivery, not condition.

N09b
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.460
It estimates a 35% chance.

N10
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.880
It still recommends filing, because the expected loss is smaller.

N11
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.370
Over the $250 line, it stops and asks, with the evidence already written.

N12a
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:05.690
The seller decides.

N12b
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.750
A yes files it, with five seconds to undo.

N13a
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.400
CaseClerk files through PayPal's Disputes API.

N13b
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.400
PayPal returns success and its own debug ID.

N14
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.790
We asked PayPal's sandbox to rule for the seller.

N15
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.400
It ruled for a seller and released the hold: $388 kept.

N16
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.100
This amount comes from PayPal's own hold and release records.

N17
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.880
You can ask the sandbox to rule for your own test case, too.

N18
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.280
The seller's limits are enforced in code, not a prompt.

N19
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.790
Evidence under $250 and refunds up to $15 go out on their own.

N20
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.900
Anything bigger waits for a yes.

N21
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.060
Under the hood, every PayPal webhook is verified first.

N22a
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.700
Claude Haiku sorts the case.

N22b
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.680
Claude Opus works with tools, and writes the evidence.

N23
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.290
Every action is checked against the limits before reaching PayPal, and AI spend is capped.

N24
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.670
Real sandbox chargebacks have run this whole loop — every answer is kept.

N25
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.660
For a one-person shop, every dispute gets an answer with every evidence ready, not a loss by default.
